&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 0&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50209115</guid><pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:58:58 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Ask HN: When can we trust AI in coding, and avoid the happy path issue?</title><link>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50209084</link><description>&lt;p&gt;I have a question, and I hope you can clarify it. Consider this scenario: Claude code runs on a desktop to develop a solution, and its directive requires Codex to test everything it does under a different directive. The Codex directive is to refute Claude's test results, maintain its own test ledger, and return PASS or NO PASS. Claude cannot negotiate; it has to fix the NO PASS issues. 
Even with such a strict environment, I am ending up with problems, and of course, when asking Claude, the answer is "This is on me; I did not do X and Y"; when I ask it whether Codex checked it, "Codex checked all but not this "...&lt;p&gt;So when can we trust the AI to do the full job, not just the happy path? 
Is there a better way to impose quality?&lt;p&gt;I feel that on large projects, even if I upload Claude directives to re-read, Claude or Codex (we can switch roles) tends to drift and miss things.&lt;/p&gt;
